# Language parity

C#, Rust, and TypeScript **must** have the same public API and runtime behavior. C# is the reference. When they diverge, change Rust and TypeScript to match C#. Do not ship a language-only helper, extra method, or auto-path.

**HARD HALT** if a Rust or TypeScript change needs an extra argument, overload, or parameter that C# does not have. Stop. Do not add `read_where`, `with_fields`, `type`, `columns`, or any other language-only argument to paper over a missing C# member. Change C# first, or stop and tell the user which C# signature is missing.

A remark that says one language does it differently is a bug. Fix the code. Do not document around the gap.

Call-site shape must match (names follow each language’s convention only: `Read` / `read` / `read`). One parameter that is query-or-where in C# is one parameter everywhere — not `read` plus `read_where`.

`Defaults` / `Create` / `Register` / `Default` mean the same thing everywhere. If env already has `INSTALLED_PROVIDERS` and `DATASOURCE_URL`, `Datasource.Default` / `Datasource.default` / `default()` applies those defaults and registers the listed factories.

```csharp
var ds = Datasource.Default;
await repo.Read(new Where(new Operator.Equals("id", 9)));
```

```rust
let ds = default()?;
repo.read_single_where(&Where::new(Operator::equals("id", 9))).await?;
```

```typescript
const ds = Datasource.default;
for await (const row of repo.read(new Where(new Operator.Equals("id", 9)))) { }
```
